  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{4}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6+(-6) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
